## Fan-Out vs Batch Workers

### Batch Workers (plural name)
Process **multiple** entities in one invocation:
- `ValidateEntities` -> validates ALL entities for a parent
- `SubmitEntities` -> submits ALL validated entities

### Fan-Out Child Workers (singular name)
Process **ONE** entity per invocation:
- `ValidateEntity` -> validates ONE entity (created by Discovery fan-out)
- `SubmitEntity` -> submits ONE entity

### Key Differences

| Aspect | Batch Worker | Fan-Out Child Worker |
|--------|--------------|---------------------|
| Input | Parent key | Single entity ID |
| Output | `{ entities: [...] }` (array) | `{ entity: {...} }` (single) |
| testOptions field | `validateEntities`, `submitEntities` | `validateEntity`, `submitEntity` |
| Failure filter | N/A | `submitEntityFailForIds` |
| Parallel execution | No (one Lambda call) | Yes (N entities = N parallel Lambdas) |

### Fan-Out Worker Failure Filtering

**CRITICAL**: Fan-out workers MUST implement failure filtering to support partial failure tests:

```typescript
const failForIds = simulatedDelays?.["submitYourItemFailForIds"] as string[] | undefined;
const shouldFail = !failForIds || failForIds.includes(entityId);
if (shouldFail) {
  await simulateFailure(...);
} else {
  logger.log(`Skipping simulated failure for ${entityId} (not in filter list)`);
}
```

**Without this filter logic**, setting `submitYourItemFailOnAttempts: [1,2,3]` would fail ALL entities instead of just the targeted ones!

